{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said, etc.</b> One must know why He changed from the customary order used throughout the Torah, ״דַּבֵּר אֶל״ etc. Further, why did He place the descriptor before the described, when He should have said ״בְּנֵי אַהֲרֹן הַכֹּהֲנִים״? And the derashot that the Sages, whose memory is for blessing, expounded in their place (Torat Kohanim) stand as they are, even though He speaks in this order. And it will be explained along the lines of what they said, whose memory is for blessing (Tanhuma here), and this is their language: The Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: It is unseemly for one who goes in and comes out before Me to gaze upon the dead, etc. — end quote. And this is His saying <b>אֱמֹר</b> — an expression of distinction. ״אֶל הַכֹּהֲנִים״ — meaning, because they are priests who minister before the King. And what is the distinction? That they shall not defile themselves for a human soul, as they said, whose memory is for blessing: it is unseemly for one who goes in and comes out, etc. And for this reason He placed the descriptor before the described, to attach the distinction to the fact that they are priests — which would not have been conveyed had He said ״אֱמֹר אֶל בְּנֵי אַהֲרֹן״." "<b>And you shall say to them: for a soul, etc.</b> One must know why He repeated to say <b>וְאָמַרְתָּ.</b> And the Sages, whose memory is for blessing, said (Torat Kohanim, Yevamot 61a) numerous derashot, yet there is still more from the Master of words. And it is along the lines of what the Rambam wrote in chapter 2 of Hilkhot Evel, and this is his language: One who defiles the priest — if both were intentional, the priest receives lashes and this one who defiled him transgresses on account of וְלִפְנֵי עִוֵּר etc., and if the priest was unwitting and the one who defiled him was intentional, this one who defiled him receives lashes — end quote. And it is difficult: from where does the Rambam know to obligate the one who defiles with lashes? For the Rav HaMaggid and the Mahariq said nothing here, other than what the Kesef Mishneh wrote, and this is its language: In Hilkhot Kilayim, our Master wrote regarding one who clothes another in kilayim — if the wearer was unwitting, the one who clothes receives lashes. And the Kesef Mishneh wondered about this and wrote that the Rosh asked this of the Rash and we have not merited a response, etc. And it is possible to him that it seems to our Master from what we say in Perek Sheloshah Minim (Nazir 44a) that Scripture reveals regarding a nazirite that the one who defiles is not treated like the one who defiled himself, since it is written ״וְטִמֵּא אֶת רֹאשׁ נִזְרוֹ״ — this implies that ordinarily we do treat the one who defiles like the one who defiled himself. Therefore our Master wrote in chapter 5 of Hilkhot Nezirut that if the nazirite was unwitting and this one who defiled him was intentional, he does not receive lashes — end of the Rav's words. And they do not appear correct, for it is difficult: first, why should the nazirite not serve as a paradigm for the entire Torah that we do not say the one who defiles is like the one who defiled himself, etc., and so on along these lines. And further, that case of the nazirite — Scripture needed to restrict there because I would have reasoned a fortiori from shaving, which is obligatory — for impurity is more stringent than shaving — therefore Scripture says ״וְטִמֵּא אֶת רֹאשׁ״ etc. — end quote. If so, without the a fortiori argument there would have been no need to restrict, and on the contrary, the force of the difficulty against the Rambam from that passage in Perek Sheloshah Minim would grow even greater." "<b>But the correct interpretation</b> in my eyes is that the Rambam's reason derives from what they said in tractate Nazir, page 44a, and this is their language: And if for impurity, etc., he did not treat the one who defiles like the one who defiled himself, then for shaving — is it not all the more so that he did not treat the one who shaves like the one who gets shaved? Scripture says ״תַּעַר לֹא יַעֲבֹר״ — read it as לֹא יַעֲבֹר: he himself shall not pass, and he shall not cause another to pass — end quote. And Rashi explained: he shall not cause another to pass over it for him — end quote. Meaning, a warning to another person not to pass a razor over the head of the nazirite; for if not, the intention would be that the nazirite shall not remove the hair of another person — and that is far-fetched. And one must know the Talmud's intention: from where does it derive ״he shall not cause another to pass over it for him״? If it derives from the Masoretic reading, behold the Masoretic form of לֹא יַעֲבֹר has a yod between the bet and the resh. And further, even if we say that the absence of the yod is not an impediment, nonetheless the word לוֹ is still missing, without which the implication of ״לֹא יַעֲבֹר״ would be that the nazirite shall not pass [a razor] over others." "<b>Rather,</b> the Talmud's intention is that it is precise about the verse's statement ״תַּעַר לֹא יַעֲבֹר״ etc. — for it should have said ״לֹא יְגַלַּח אֶת רֹאשׁוֹ״ or ״לֹא יַעֲבִיר עַל רֹאשׁוֹ״ with a yod between the bet and the resh, and it would refer back to the nazirite mentioned just above, and it would hang the prohibition as commanded upon him, not upon the act of the razor inapplicable to the one being shaved — as in His saying ״תַּעַר לֹא יַעֲבֹר,״ the plain sense of which declares that He prohibited the act whether performed by the one being shaved or by others. And this is the Talmud's statement: read it as לֹא יַעֲבֹר — he himself shall not pass, and he shall not cause another to pass over it for him — meaning, the implication of the word declares to prohibit the matter whether by his own hand or by the hand of another. And the statement ״read it as״ is not precise, but rather they clothe the intended meaning in the word — and understand." "And from now on, likewise we may say regarding the law of kilayim, of which He said (Leviticus 19:19) ״וּבֶגֶד כִּלְאַיִם שַׁעַטְנֵז לֹא יַעֲלֶה עָלֶיךָ״ — and He did not say ״לֹא תַעֲלֶה עָלֶיךָ״ — this implies that He did not command only the one who puts it on, but rather He commanded regarding the act itself, that it shall not occur whether by him or by another. Therefore the Rambam ruled (Hilkhot Kilayim 10:1) that one who clothes another in kilayim intentionally receives lashes. And likewise we may say regarding that which is before us, the impurity of a priest: from the verse's saying ״לֹא יִטַּמָּא״ — a warning inapplicable to the one becoming defiled, meaning that it warns both the priest himself and others regarding the impurity. And the measure of the mitzvah is that HaShem blessed be He commands that the priest not be impure. And from now on, whoever defiles him — whether he himself or another — has transgressed the negative commandment and receives lashes. And according to this, the Rambam's rulings are correct." "<b>And with this</b> we shall come to settle the fine point of the verse, which doubled to say ״אֱמֹר״ and ״אָמַרְתָּ״ — for on the side that HaShem commanded the priests and also others concerning their impurity, with respect to the priests He said ״אֱמֹר אֶל הַכֹּהֲנִים״, and with respect to the warning to others not to defile them, He said <b>וְאָמַרְתָּ</b> to them. Or perhaps the word ״אֲלֵיהֶם״ refers back to Israel, who are commanded in the entire commandment. And you will find that at the end of this matter He said (Leviticus 21:24): ״וַיְדַבֵּר מֹשֶׁה אֶל אַהֲרֹן וְאֶל בָּנָיו וְאֶל כָּל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — behold, the commandment was stated from HaShem also to Israel, and this justifies for you our interpretation that the warning here comes also to Israel not to defile the priests. And in Torat Kohanim (Leviticus 21:24) they said, and this is their language: ״וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳״ — He warned Aaron through the sons, and the sons through Israel, and the sons one through the other. And these and those are words of the living Elohim." "<b>לְנֶפֶשׁ.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory darshaned (Torat Kohanim) to include a quarter-log of blood, etc. And I saw the Rambam, of blessed memory, who wrote in chapter 3 of Hilkhot Evel, and this is his language: ״אֶחָד הַמֵּת וְאֶחָד שְׁאָר הַטֻּמְאוֹת הַפּוֹרְשׁוֹת מִן הַמֵּת שֶׁנֶּאֱמַר לְנֶפֶשׁ״, end of quote. And they objected to him from what the Tanna taught in Torat Kohanim, and this is their language: ״אֵין לִי אֶלָּא הַמֵּת, מִנַּיִן לְרַבּוֹת הַדָּם? תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר לְנֶפֶשׁ. מִנַּיִן לְרַבּוֹת כָּל הַטֻּמְאוֹת הַפּוֹרְשׁוֹת מִן הַמֵּת? תַּלְמוּד לוֹמַר וְאָמַרְתָּ אֲלֵיהֶם לְרַבּוֹת״, end of quote. And the Kesef Mishneh answered that the Rambam holds that from the word ״לְנֶפֶשׁ״ we derive everything, for they are equivalent, and the verse is needed only as an asmakhta, end of quote. And the words of the Kesef Mishneh do not appear [correct], for from where does the Rambam have [authority] to construct a new structure contrary to the words of the baraita?" "<b>However,</b> the correct [resolution] is that the Rambam, of blessed memory, saw a different baraita, and behold it is written in the Pesikta, and this is their language: ״אֱמוֹר וְאָמַרְתָּ״ — to warn the great about the small; ״לְנֶפֶשׁ לֹא יִטַּמָּא״ — to bring a quarter-log of blood that issues from the dead, which is included in ״לֹא יִטַּמָּא״, end of their language. And this baraita is brought in Yevamot (104a). And according to this baraita you must say that also the other impurities that depart from the dead are included from ״לְנֶפֶשׁ״, for behold it darshans the doubling ״וְאָמַרְתָּ״ to warn the great about the small. And the matter is clear to me that this baraita was hidden from the eyes of the Kesef Mishneh, and the proof is that the Rav wrote in the aforementioned chapter, law 12, and this is his language: ״כֹּהֵן קָטָן״ etc. ״הַגְּדוֹלִים״ etc. ״כְּפֵרוּשׁ רַשִׁי״ ״אֱמוֹר וְאָמַרְתָּ״ etc., end of quote. Behold, from the fact that he proves this law from the words of Rashi and not from the words of the baraita, you know that he did not know the baraita." "<b>And there remains</b> to settle: from where do they have [basis] to darshan from the word ״לְנֶפֶשׁ״ a quarter-log of blood, etc.? For perhaps ״לְנֶפֶשׁ״ was needed to inform that He warns only concerning corpse-impurity and not concerning other impurities. And one may say that He should have said ״לְמֵת לֹא יִטַּמָּא״, and from His saying <b>לְנֶפֶשׁ</b> they darshaned a quarter-log of blood and everything that has נֶפֶשׁ in it. But those impure through [contact with] a corpse are not included in this." "<b>לֹא יִטַּמָּא.</b> It used singular language, even though it began to speak in plural language — ״הַכֹּהֲנִים״, ״אֲלֵיהֶם״. According to the reasoning of the Tanna who darshaned the doubling ״וְאָמַרְתָּ״ to warn the great about the small, from now on we have no locus of warning for the one who defiles not to defile a priest. Therefore it said singular language and address to the plural, to say that the warning of the many is upon the individual — from this you have learned a warning for the one who defiles." "<b>Further</b>, it appears that the verse was concerned to say ״לֹא יִטַּמָּאוּ״, lest the reasoning err and suppose that the verse was particular only about impurity of the collectivity of the priests — for this matter is nothing but a decree (חֻקָּה), and we have only what is stated explicitly — but an individual priest it would not hold accountable. Therefore Scripture teaches ״יִטַּמָּא״ in the singular." "Further, inasmuch as HaShem commanded the priests (Leviticus 10:9) the prohibition of wine and strong drink, and there the verse disclosed that the reason for the prohibition is lest they perform sacred service while drunk on wine — if so, when it is not the time of their watch or not the time of service they may drink wine and strong drink, and if so the reasoning would come to infer the unclear from the explicit, judging the reason for the prohibition of impurity by the same reason itself: just as drinking wine and strong drink was forbidden, so impurity is forbidden in order that one be fit for service. Therefore HaShem in His wisdom spoke in the singular: ״לֹא יִטַּמָּא״, to say that even one of the priests stands under this prohibition. And from the implication of the matter you learn that it is not for the reason of nullification of service, for service is not nullified by the absence of a single priest; rather, it is certainly an obligation on the individual person (חוֹבַת גַּבְרָא). And even one whose watch comes at a distant time is nevertheless under this prohibition — for the reason stated by the Sages of blessed memory, and we have written it above."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:01:10.199247627Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}